{"folioDeck": 1, "exportedAt": "2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z", "meta": {"id": "zuphrase", "title": "Zulu Phrases", "subtitle": "99 phrases and sentences · 33 phrases, 66 sentences · both directions", "desc": "Zulu said whole: the greetings and their answers, the goodbyes, the phrasebook formulas a visitor needs and a handful of everyday sentences. Both study directions, each a card of its own: Zulu → English and English → Zulu. 33 PHRASES are every phrase and interjection English Wiktionary has for Zulu that the level decks do not teach, with Wiktionary's own meaning: unjani 'how are you?' (to one person) and ninjani (to several), the goodbyes — hamba kahle 'go well' to the one leaving, sala kahle 'stay well' to the one staying, each with its plural — ngubani igama lakho 'what is your name?', angiqondi 'I don't understand', the exclamations hawu and maye, and the saying umuntu ngumuntu ngabantu, 'a person is a person through other people'. 66 SENTENCES are Tatoeba's short Zulu sentences (nine words at most) with their English translation, each card giving the sentence's Tatoeba number. THE DECK IS SMALL BECAUSE THE SOURCES ARE: Wiktionary files 46 Zulu entries as phrases or interjections and Tatoeba holds about a hundred Zulu sentences, so nothing here is invented and nothing is padded. It is not a CEFR level. sawubona 'hello', yebo and cha are in A1, and nothing the level decks teach is repeated here. A speaker reads each card where the device has a Zulu voice. Sources: English Wiktionary, via the kaikki.org extraction (CC BY-SA 4.0); Tatoeba (tatoeba.org), CC BY 2.0 FR.", "author": "", "language": "en", "color": "#9A012F", "tags": ["zulu", "isizulu", "phrases", "greetings", "sentences"], "glossMode": "site", "types": {"zulu": {"id": "zulu", "name": "Zulu vocabulary", "speechLang": "zu-ZA", "fields": ["Zulu", "Tone", "Word", "Say", "English", "Forms", "Table", "Examples"], "cards": [{"name": "Zulu → English", "front": "<div class=\"uc-word\">{{Zulu}}<span class=\"uc-tt